The '''Farta Geedi''' ([[Burawi language|Burawi]]: "Geedi script") was a {{W|logographic}} writing system developed in the [[Kingdom of Ufyen]] during the [[Geedi Dynasty]] (~1,000–500 BCE). It is the earliest known indigenous writing system of [[Southwestern Kaftia]]. Originally used for religious inscriptions and royal records at the court of [[Badalga-Jir]], the script rose to prominence as the administrative writing system of the Ufyen state before being gradually supplanted by the [[Mesogean script|Echian script]] following [[Aetolians|Aetolian]] contact (~600 BCE). By approximately 200 BCE, the Farta Geedi had fallen out of regular use, surviving only in conservative ritual contexts at the [[Buur Waaq]] shrine.

== Origins ==

The Farta Geedi appears to have originated in a religious context. The earliest attested examples are carved symbols on stone slabs at the summit shrine of [[Buur Waaq]] and at the temple platforms of [[Ufyen (city)|Ufyen]], dating to the early Geedi period (~1,000–800 BCE). These early inscriptions consist of short sequences of symbols accompanying sacrificial offerings and are interpreted as dedicatory formulae addressed to [[Waaq]].

The system was logographic: each symbol represented a word or concept rather than a sound. Early symbols depicted recognisable objects — cattle, grain sheaves, mountains, the sun disc (representing Waaq) — in a stylised pictographic form. As the system developed, more abstract symbols were added to represent actions, quantities, and relationships, though the script never developed a systematic means of representing individual sounds or syllables.

== Administrative use ==

Under the late Geedi Dynasty (~800–500 BCE), the Farta Geedi was adopted for secular administrative purposes. Scribes at [[Badalga-Jir]] and [[Jowhar]] used the script to record tribute tallies, inventory lists, and royal decrees. Carved stone tablets bearing Farta Geedi inscriptions have been recovered from ''[[Kaas|kaas]]'' forts along the [[Buran River]] valley, indicating that the script was used for record-keeping at garrison outposts as well as at the capital.

The logographic nature of the script limited its practical utility. Recording a simple trade transaction required the scribe to know hundreds of distinct symbols, and the system lacked a consistent method for rendering personal names, foreign words, or abstract concepts that did not correspond to an existing logograph. These limitations were of little consequence in the closed administrative environment of the Ufyen court, where scribes were trained from childhood, but they made the script poorly suited to the demands of long-distance commerce.

== Decline ==

The arrival of [[Aetolians|Aetolian]] traders on the southern coast around 600 BCE introduced the [[Mesogean script|Echian script]], an alphabetic writing system developed in the [[Kingdom of Echia]]. The Echian script represented individual sounds rather than whole words, making it simpler to learn, faster to write, and capable of rendering any language. Coastal merchants adopted the Echian script for commercial record-keeping within a generation of first contact.

The Echian script spread inland through the trade networks linking the coast to the highlands. By the [[Dalmar Dynasty]] (~400 BCE), the Ufyen court itself had adopted the Echian script for diplomatic correspondence, legal codes, and administrative records. The Farta Geedi was progressively confined to religious contexts — inscriptions at temples and shrines, ritual formulae, and the coronation rites at [[Buur Waaq]]. By approximately 200 BCE, even these functions were increasingly performed in the Echian script, and knowledge of the Farta Geedi was limited to a small number of priests at the highland shrines.

== Decipherment ==

The Farta Geedi was effectively a dead script by the beginning of the common era. Knowledge of its symbols survived in fragmentary form through the ritual traditions of the [[Buur Waaq]] priesthood, but no continuous tradition of reading connected to a broader understanding of the writing system.

Modern scholarship has identified approximately 350 distinct Farta Geedi logographs from surviving inscriptions. Of these, roughly 180 have been assigned probable meanings through comparison with later Echian-script translations of similar texts and through analysis of the pictographic elements of the symbols. The remaining logographs are undeciphered.

== Legacy ==

Although the Farta Geedi was replaced as a functional writing system, several of its symbols survived as decorative or symbolic motifs in later Burawi culture. The sun disc of Waaq, the mountain glyph, and the "flowing water" symbol appear in the architectural ornamentation of medieval [[Kingdom of Burawa|Burawi]] buildings and on ceremonial objects. The script's existence also demonstrates that the southern highlands developed indigenous literacy independently of Mesogean influence, a point of significance in the historiography of [[Southwestern Kaftia]].

